\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs28\b BULK NOTE EDITING 1.6 (PARATEXT PLUGIN, EXPERIMENTAL)\b0\fs22\par
\par
\b PURPOSE\b0\par
Bulk Note Editing is a Paratext plugin for handling many project notes at once. It lists the notes of a verse, a chapter, a whole book or the whole project, of one project or several together, lets you narrow the list with filters (status, read or not, who wrote the note, who commented, who it is assigned to, dates, words, tag), and resolves or assigns all the notes you tick in one step.\par
This tool is experimental. Try it first on a project where a mistake does no harm.\par
Notes cannot be deleted with this tool: Paratext does not let plugins delete notes.\par
\par
\b INSTALLING\b0\par
1. Download Install_Phils_tools.exe from the Paratext_Plugins folder. If your browser warns about the file, choose to keep it.\par
2. Close Paratext.\par
3. Double-click Install_Phils_tools.exe. If Windows says it protected your PC, click "More info", then "Run anyway" (the installer is not signed yet).\par
4. Tick Bulk Note Editing (experimental) and click "Install / update ticked". It installs into every Paratext 9 on the computer. If Windows asks for permission, click Yes. The same window updates the plugin later (its status then says "Update available") or removes it ("Remove ticked"), and its "User guide" button opens this guide.\par
\par
\b HOW TO USE\b0\par
1. Open the project and choose Tools > Bulk Note Editing... The window opens at the book, chapter and verse you are looking at. "Projects" names the projects listed: click it to tick several projects and list their notes together; a Project column then shows where each note is. The same book, chapter and verse are read in each project. The tool remembers the projects you ticked, and always adds the project you start it from.\par
2. In "Show", choose Verse, Chapter, Whole book or Whole project, then the book, chapter and verse. "< Previous" and "Next >" move by verse, chapter or book. A large project can take a moment to read as a whole. With "Follow Paratext" ticked, the window moves to the place you go to in Paratext. A note on a range of verses (for example MRK 2:10-12) is listed at each of its verses.\par
3. Narrow the list with the filters:\par
\tab Status: Unresolved (the default), Resolved or All.\par
\tab Read: Read or not (the default), Unread or Read, for you. With Status Resolved and Read Unread, the list shows the notes that were resolved but that you have not read yet. A note counts as read once you have opened it in Paratext's Notes window, or marked it read with this tool (step 8). Notes you resolve or assign with this tool are marked read too.\par
\tab Created by: the person who wrote the note (its first comment).\par
\tab Comment by: anyone who wrote any comment of the note.\par
\tab Assigned to: a person, Team, or (nobody).\par
\tab Dates: tick "from" and/or "to" to limit the date the note was created, or the date of its last comment.\par
\tab Words: notes with these words in any comment, or attached to these words.\par
\tab Tag: notes with this tag. The list has the project's tags, then Paratext's own: Conflict (the notes Send/Receive makes when two people changed the same verse), Spelling and Biblical term. A note with no tag counts as To Do, as in Paratext. With several projects, a tag is found by its name, because each project numbers its own tags.\par
"Clear filters" removes them all. Click a column heading to sort by that column; click again to reverse the order.\par
"Hide filters" hides the filter boxes to leave more room for the list; "Show filters" brings them back, and the tool remembers your choice. The line beside the button always says which filters are in effect.\par
Colours in the list: unresolved conflict notes are light red; notes made in the Wordlist or in Biblical Terms are yellow; other resolved notes are light green. Notes you have not read are in bold, and the Read column says Yes or No. A key below the list shows the colours.\par
4. Click a note to see all its comments below the list. "Go to in Paratext" (or a double-click) shows the verse in Paratext.\par
5. Tick the notes to change, or click "Tick all listed".\par
6. If you want, type a comment in "Comment to add". It is added to each note.\par
7. Click "Resolve ticked", or choose a person in "Assign to" and click "Assign ticked". "Assign to" offers Team, the people still active in the project (users whose role in Paratext's list of project users is not None) and (nobody). With several projects it offers anyone active in at least one of them: a note in a project where that person is not active is left as it is and listed in Results. The tool asks before it changes anything.\par
8. "Mark ticked as read" and "Mark ticked as unread" mark the ticked notes as read or unread for you, without adding a comment. Only your own read status changes: Paratext keeps it on your computer, outside the project, and does not share it by Send/Receive, so it works even where you are an observer. It uses a part of Paratext that is not officially offered to plugins: if your version of Paratext does not allow it, nothing is changed and Results says so. Paratext's Notes window may show the change only after you open it again.\par
